course: euphemisms
lesson: 33
title: Soft words for hard things: failure and mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nobody likes to say "we failed" or "I made a mistake", so people reach for softer words: a company whose new phone caught fire may call it "a hiccup", which makes a big failure sound as small as a hiccup.
explain: Teachers and bosses use gentle words to be kind, such as "room for improvement" on a school report, which really means the work is poor.
explain: Officials and businesses use words to hide or spin: "human error" hides who exactly made the mistake, and "a correction" makes a crash in prices sound like a neat tidy-up.
explain: Friends use jokey slang to laugh at their own mistakes: "it all went pear-shaped" or "I had a brain fart".
fact: "Didn't go to plan" means failed or went wrong.
fact: "Not our finest hour" is a humorous way of saying a bad failure or embarrassment.
fact: "A setback" means a failure, made to sound like a small step back.
fact: "A hiccup" means a problem or failure, made to sound small.
fact: "A glitch" means a fault or bug, usually in a machine or computer.
fact: "An oversight" means a mistake caused by carelessness, something someone forgot or missed.
fact: "Human error" (official) means someone made a mistake, without saying who.
fact: "A brain fart" (slang) means a silly mistake or a moment of forgetting.
fact: "A slip of the tongue" means saying the wrong word by mistake.
fact: "A misstep" means a mistake.
fact: "Room for improvement" means the work is poor.
fact: "Needs improvement", on a school report, means the work is poor or failing.
fact: "Below expectations" (official) means poor, worse than it should be.
fact: "Underperforming" (jargon) means doing badly.
fact: "A correction", on the stock market (where shares, small parts of companies, are bought and sold), means a sharp fall in prices.
fact: "Challenging" (jargon) means bad or very difficult.
fact: "Gone pear-shaped" (UK slang) means gone wrong.
fact: "Gone sideways" (US slang) means gone wrong.
fact: "Gone south" (US slang) means gone wrong or got worse.
fact: "Came a cropper" (UK) means failed badly or fell over.
exercise: What does "the school trip didn't go to plan" mean? | Things went wrong on the trip | The trip was planned again | The trip had no plan at all | The trip went to a different country on purpose
exercise: The captain says, "That match was not our finest hour." What does she mean? | They played very badly that day | They played for less than an hour | They played their best ever | They played early in the morning
exercise: What does "the project has had a setback" mean? | The project has failed in some way | The project has been set up | The project has been moved back a room | The project has been finished early
exercise: A company says, "There was a small hiccup with the new trains." What really happened? | The trains had a problem | A driver had the hiccups | The trains made funny noises | The trains arrived early
exercise: What does "there's a glitch in the game" mean? | The game has a fault or bug | The game has a secret level | The game is very shiny | The game is brand new
exercise: "Leaving your name off the list was an oversight." What happened? | Someone carelessly forgot your name | Someone looked over your name | Someone left your name off on purpose | Someone wrote your name too big
exercise: What does an airline mean when it blames a mix-up on "human error"? | Someone made a mistake | A robot made a mistake | Nobody made any mistake | The weather caused it
exercise: Your friend says, "sorry, total brain fart, forgot your birthday." What does she mean? | She had a silly moment of forgetting | Her brain hurt badly | She was ill on your birthday | She forgot on purpose
exercise: What is "a slip of the tongue"? | Saying the wrong word by mistake | Biting your tongue | Slipping on a wet floor | Sticking your tongue out
exercise: A politician calls a bad decision "a misstep". What does he mean? | It was a wrong move | It was a dance step | He tripped on a stair | It was a clever move
exercise: What does "there is room for improvement" on your report mean? | Your work is not yet good | Your classroom is too small | Your work is excellent | You need a bigger desk
exercise: What does "Handwriting: needs improvement" on a report mean? | Your handwriting is poor | Your handwriting is very good | You need a new pen | You write too slowly
exercise: A boss writes "below expectations" in a worker's review. What does it mean? | The work was not good enough | The work was underground | The work was better than expected | The work was not checked
exercise: What does "the shop is underperforming" mean? | The shop is doing badly | The shop is under a bridge | The shop puts on shows | The shop is doing very well
exercise: What is "a market correction" in the news? | A sharp fall in prices | A teacher marking mistakes | A new market opening | A fix to a spelling error
exercise: What does "it has been a challenging year for the club" usually mean? | It has been a hard, bad year | It has been a year of games and contests | It has been an easy year | It has been a lucky year
exercise: What does "the party went pear-shaped" mean in Britain? | The party went badly wrong | The party served pears | The party had a fruit theme | The party was round and fat
exercise: What does "the deal went sideways" mean in the US? | The deal went badly wrong | The deal moved to a new office | The deal was signed sideways | The deal went very well
exercise: What does "the business went south" mean? | The business got worse | The business moved to the south | The business had a holiday | The business grew fast
exercise: What does "he came a cropper on his new skates" mean? | He fell over badly | He won a prize | He cut the grass | He skated very well
test: News: "A computer glitch left 2,000 passengers stuck at the airport." What caused the trouble? | A fault in the computers | A strike by pilots | Bad weather | Too many passengers
test: "Your homework shows room for improvement, Sam." What is the teacher telling Sam? | His homework is not good enough | His homework is perfect | He needs a bigger room | He should do less homework
test: "Mate, the barbecue went proper pear-shaped, sausages all burnt." What happened? | The barbecue went wrong | They cooked pears | The barbecue was a great success | The sausages were shaped like pears
test: A bank says: "Due to human error, some customers were charged twice." What happened? | A worker made a mistake | Customers made a mistake | A machine broke on purpose | Nothing went wrong at all
test: "I called my teacher 'Mum' — just a slip of the tongue, but everyone laughed." What happened? | She said the wrong word by mistake | She fell over in class | She bit her tongue | She was rude on purpose
test: The minister calls the failed bridge project "a minor setback". What should a careful listener think? | The project failed and he is making it sound small | The project is going perfectly | The bridge was moved back a bit | The bridge is finished
test: Tara says: "'The market had a correction' means it fixed its mistakes, yeah?" Is she right? | No, it means prices fell sharply | Yes, it fixed its spelling | Yes, it got more customers | No, it means the market closed
test: "ugh, the whole plan went south when the car broke down" What happened to the plan? | It went wrong | They drove south | It got better | It was finished early
test: Report card: "Maths: below expectations." How is the pupil doing in maths? | Badly | Very well | About right | It has not been marked
test: "Our team came a cropper in the final, lost 6–0. Not our finest hour, ha." How did the team do? | They lost badly | They won the cup | They drew the match | They did not play
test: "sorry I said 7 × 8 is 54, total brain fart, it's 56" What was the mistake? | A silly moment of forgetting | A rude joke | A brain illness | A trick on purpose
test: Boss on the phone: "The meeting went sideways, the client walked out." How did the meeting go? | Badly | Very well | Nobody came | It was moved to a new room
exam: In respect of an organisation's statement that a data loss was attributable to "human error", identify the meaning of the expression. | A mistake was committed by a person whose identity is not disclosed | The fault originated exclusively within automated machinery | No mistake whatsoever was committed | The loss was occasioned by adverse weather
exam: Whereas a financial bulletin reports "a correction" in share prices, which of the following statements most accurately characterises the event? | Share prices declined sharply | Errors in the published figures were rectified | Share prices rose to an unprecedented level | Trading was suspended for administrative reasons
exam: It is given that a pupil's report states that the pupil's written work "needs improvement". Ascertain the assessment conveyed thereby. | The work is of an unsatisfactory standard | The work is of an exemplary standard | The pupil requires additional stationery | The pupil has completed an excessive quantity of work
exam: Determine the meaning of the expression "gone pear-shaped" as employed colloquially in the United Kingdom. | Gone wrong | Become rounded in form | Been supplemented with fruit | Proceeded according to plan
